WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.268 line:92%
-The daily briefings are..

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:02.736 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
a circus.

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:06.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot has been written
and said about...

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:09.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether or not they should
even be televised.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.245
How do you -- Are you gettin
any information from them?

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:14.314 size:78% position:39%
Do you feel as though
it's fair criticism

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:16.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
to say they maybe
should be on TV less?

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, I think -- Yeah.
I think that the --

00:00:18.952 --> 00:00:21.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I think,
the critique, to me,

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:24.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
the concern I have
is that they often --

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.293
they literally communicate
public health information

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:31.031
that's wrong, or is in contrast,
or tension,

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the best
available evidence.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:35.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that can --
That's dangerous, right?

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:35.668 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I mean...

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:39.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
we know there's been
a sort of partisan divide

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
about how seriously
people take this,

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:42.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
how much they're adhering
to social distancing.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
Though that's closed quite
a bit, which is good.

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
But that, to me,
is the real concern about this

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:49.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
I do think that the briefings,
as a performance,

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:54.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
have kind of entered their
like, baroque phase.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:55.622 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughs ]

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:58.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
The writers are out of ideas
and it's -- you know --

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a moment yesterday
where he was --

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
the president of
the United States --

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:04.964 align:start size:94% position:6%
2,000 Americans
died yesterday,

00:01:04.964 --> 00:01:07.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
nearly 30,000 Americans
have passed away.

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.103
The economy is contracting
at a Great Depression level.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:12.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the president,
for three minutes,

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
just read the names
of companies.
-Yeah.

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:17.510 size:78% position:39%
-Like, "Chik-fil-A --
we love Chik-fil-A.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
McDonald's."
-Yeah.

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.247 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-[ Stammers ]

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:26.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
To me, the more obviously
they are ridiculous exercise

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
in his own
self-aggrandizement,

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:32.225 align:start size:94% position:6%
the weirdly less dangerous
they become.

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:34.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
The earlier phases
felt more dangerous to me

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they had
the putative frame

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:40.600
of being a briefing from
the task force on public health.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
At this point, it seems to m
that it's so obvious

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's not what's --
That's not what's happening.

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:50.777
-One of the biggest shifts
that is happening, as well

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
is this idea of reopening
the economy.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:55.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
It seems like -- and again
it changes every day --

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
it seems as though
the president is fixating

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:59.385 align:start size:94% position:6%
on this idea
of May 1st.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:01.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
And how much of this,
do you feel,

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:05.391 size:78% position:39%
is driven by his fear
of a failing economy

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
versus his sense
that we're all safe

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
to go back
to our lives?

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, it's 100%
the former.

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:12.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
The biggest mistake they mad
from the beginning --

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:13.967 size:88% position:44%
and I talked about this
on my show last night --

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:18.204
was, in the beginning, when th
problem began to materialize

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:21.574
and manifest -- and particularly
manifest in the stock market -

00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.544
the president clearly thought,
"I'm gonna run for re-election

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
on a great,
booming economy."

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
They were explicit
about that.

00:02:27.247 --> 00:02:28.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's not like --
That's what they said.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
And now, "The economy's
doing poorly.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.318 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's gonna hurt
my re-election.

00:02:32.318 --> 00:02:33.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
We need to pump
the economy back up."

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.222
And at the beginning,
they were focused on the economy

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:39.859
as a somehow separate proble
from the public health problem

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:43.463
And the thing experts kept
saying is like, "No, no, no.

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:44.831 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not --
You've got to deal with

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:47.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
the public heath problem.
You can't just..."

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.003
And eventually, I think
he was convinced of that,

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.938 size:81% position:41%
and now, is going back
to the old thinking,

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.607
which is, "Okay, now we're gonna
reopen the economy."

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's still the same
Goddamn virus.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.613
It's the same virus that's
you know, circling around.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's still the same thing
causing the problems

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
to have us lock down
the economy.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:09.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's no independent solutio
to reopening the economy

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:12.559 align:start size:94% position:6%
that is independent
from a plan to deal with

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:13.293 size:81% position:41% line:92%
and contain the virus.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:14.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're gonna be right back
with more Chris Hayes.

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:17.830
And I want to talk about the
fact that there is an election

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though no one
is talking about it.

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:22.602 size:75% position:38% line:92%
We'll be right back.

